## Definitions, conventions and mathematical basis

For every real x:
- [x] = floor(x), the largest integer not exceeding x.
- {x} = x - [x].
- [x] <= x < [x]+1; consequently 0 <= {x} < 1.
- x = [x]+{x}, with [x] an integer and {x} in [0,1).

These are the original prose definitions and their direct consequences.
Raw zero-based paragraph locations are 1 (floor definition), 2 (notation),
8 (universal bounds), 12 (fractional-part definition), 13 (notation),
14 (difference formula), 17 (fractional-part range), 18 (decomposition),
and 23 (general floor interval rule). They are locations in the raw snapshot,
not finalized modern concept selectors. No concept block has been prepared.
Solved-example paragraphs 0, 4, 16, 20 and the illustrative cases 22, 31,
32, 33 are NOT being copied or designated as concept definitions.
The noninteger-only strict bound in paragraph 6 must not be applied at integers.

Negative convention: floor is not truncation toward zero. If m is an integer
and m < x < m+1, then [x]=m even when x<0. A negative noninteger has a
nonnegative fractional part, obtained by subtracting that lower integer.
An integer, including zero and negative integers, has fractional part zero.
For a positive noninteger t with fractional part r, 0<r<1,
[-t]=-[t]-1 and {-t}=1-r; for integer t the extra -1 is absent.

Printed interval [n,n+1[ is written here as [n,n+1): left endpoint included,
right endpoint excluded. Unless explicitly constrained by an interval, x ranges
over R; all auxiliary n and k used as integer parameters range over Z.
Finite decimals in these tasks are exact rational numbers, not measurements.
The printed negative mixed number -2 9/23 means -(2+9/23), not -2+9/23.

Uniqueness of decomposition: if x=m+r with m in Z and 0<=r<1, then
m<=x<m+1, so m=[x] and r={x}. Merely writing x as two summands does not
meet the decomposition task if these conditions fail.

## 653: integer parts of all eight given numbers

Task: determine the largest integer not exceeding each given number.

| Part | Source x | Bounding unit interval | [x] |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | 1.8 | 1 <= 1.8 < 2 | 1 |
| b | 263.4 | 263 <= 263.4 < 264 | 263 |
| c | 27 | 27 <= 27 < 28 | 27 |
| d | 0.345 | 0 <= 0.345 < 1 | 0 |
| e | -0.4 | -1 <= -0.4 < 0 | -1 |
| f | -0.97 | -1 <= -0.97 < 0 | -1 |
| g | -35.2 | -36 <= -35.2 < -35 | -36 |
| h | -10.7 | -11 <= -10.7 < -10 | -11 |

Complete ordered result: (1,263,27,0,-1,-1,-36,-11).
The integer 27 remains 27; both negative numbers between -1 and 0 have floor
-1. The negative multi-digit cases also require moving downward, not just
removing decimal digits. These are the substantive distinctions to retain.

