## 655: fractional parts of all eight given numbers

Task: determine x-[x], not a signed decimal tail.

| Part | Source x | Bound for [x] | [x] | Exact subtraction and {x} |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | 3.6 | 3 <= x < 4 | 3 | 3.6-3 = 0.6 |
| b | 10 | 10 <= x < 11 | 10 | 10-10 = 0 |
| c | 0.64 | 0 <= x < 1 | 0 | 0.64-0 = 0.64 |
| d | -0.64 | -1 <= x < 0 | -1 | -0.64-(-1) = 0.36 |
| e | 6.27 | 6 <= x < 7 | 6 | 6.27-6 = 0.27 |
| f | -6.27 | -7 <= x < -6 | -7 | -6.27-(-7) = 0.73 |
| g | -100.01 | -101 <= x < -100 | -101 | -100.01-(-101) = 0.99 |
| h | -7/11 | -1=-11/11 <= -7/11 < 0 | -1 | -7/11-(-1) = 4/11 |

Complete ordered result: (0.6,0,0.64,0.36,0.27,0.73,0.99,4/11).
Every result is in [0,1). The positive/negative 0.64 and 6.27 pairs have
complementary nonzero fractional parts. The rational case h is exact and is
not replaced by a rounded decimal.

## 656: evaluate all four brace expressions

Task: interpret the braces as fractional-part notation.

| Part | Expression | Floor determination | Exact value |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | {9.18} | 9 <= 9.18 < 10, [9.18]=9 | 9.18-9 = 0.18 |
| b | {45} | 45 <= 45 < 46, [45]=45 | 45-45 = 0 |
| c | {0.25} | 0 <= 0.25 < 1, [0.25]=0 | 0.25-0 = 0.25 |
| d | {-134.8} | -135 <= -134.8 < -134, [-134.8]=-135 | -134.8-(-135) = 0.2 |

Complete ordered result: (0.18,0,0.25,0.2).
The negative case is a difference from the lower integer; its result is not
-0.8, 0.8 or a signed remainder.

## 657: determine the pair ([x],{x}) for both signs

Task a: x=65.4. Since 65<=65.4<66, [x]=65 and {x}=65.4-65=0.4.
Task b: x=-65.4. Since -66<=-65.4<-65, [x]=-66 and
{x}=-65.4-(-66)=0.6.

Complete paired result:
- x=65.4: ([x],{x})=(65,0.4); 65.4=65+0.4.
- x=-65.4: ([x],{x})=(-66,0.6); -65.4=-66+0.6.

Negating x does not simply negate its floor and preserve its decimal digits:
the floor changes by the extra -1 and the nonzero fractional parts complement
to 1. Both coordinates of each pair must belong to the same x.

## 658: all eight required integer-plus-fractional decompositions

Task: express each source number as its own integer part plus its own
fractional part; verify both the sum and the range restrictions.

| Part | Source x | Bound for floor | Required decomposition |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | 21.43 | 21 <= x < 22 | 21.43 = 21+0.43 |
| b | 0.57 | 0 <= x < 1 | 0.57 = 0+0.57 |
| c | 14.5 | 14 <= x < 15 | 14.5 = 14+0.5 |
| d | -14.5 | -15 <= x < -14 | -14.5 = -15+0.5 |
| e | -0.068 | -1 <= x < 0 | -0.068 = -1+0.932 |
| f | -1.05 | -2 <= x < -1 | -1.05 = -2+0.95 |
| g | -5.44 | -6 <= x < -5 | -5.44 = -6+0.56 |
| h | -(2+9/23) = -55/23 | -69/23 <= -55/23 < -46/23 | -(2+9/23) = -3+14/23 |

For h: -55/23-(-3)=(-55+69)/23=14/23, strictly between 0 and 1.
For e: -0.068+1=0.932; for f: -1.05+2=0.95; for g:
-5.44+6=0.56. Each first summand is the floor and each second summand lies
in [0,1), so uniqueness proves all eight decompositions meet the task.
A signed tail or an arbitrary two-term sum is not a permissible replacement.

## 659: several independently constructed examples for every target floor

Task: produce several numbers for each specified integer part. The task is
open: there is no unique finite list. Its complete admissible family is needed
to justify and vary examples rather than pretending one sample is exhaustive.

For any integer n, [x]=n if and only if x=n+t with 0<=t<1, equivalently
x in [n,n+1). Choose t=0,1/4,9/10 to give three distinct valid examples
for each of the six actual targets.

| Part | Target floor n | Complete admissible set | Several valid examples |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | 14 | [14,15) | 14, 14.25, 14.9 |
| b | 1 | [1,2) | 1, 1.25, 1.9 |
| c | 0 | [0,1) | 0, 0.25, 0.9 |
| d | -1 | [-1,0) | -1, -0.75, -0.1 |
| e | -5 | [-5,-4) | -5, -4.75, -4.1 |
| f | -10 | [-10,-9) | -10, -9.75, -9.1 |

For every entry, subtracting the target n gives respectively 0,0.25,0.9,
all in [0,1). Thus n<=x<n+1 and the floor equals the requested n.
The left endpoint n itself is allowed; n+1 is excluded because its floor is
n+1. For a negative target, examples run to the right of n, not to the left:
-4.75 is in [-5,-4) and has floor -5.
These are source-task solutions, not proposed choice alternatives.
The infinite interval family remains part of the mathematical understanding;
recognizing offered examples later would not prove independently producing them.

## 660: complete sets for all five given floor values

Task: determine the entire real solution set, not just sample witnesses.
Apply [x]=n iff n<=x<n+1 separately to all specified n.

| Part | Given [x] | Complete condition | Complete solution set |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | 8 | 8 <= x < 9 | [8,9) |
| b | 1 | 1 <= x < 2 | [1,2) |
| c | 0 | 0 <= x < 1 | [0,1) |
| d | -1 | -1 <= x < 0 | [-1,0) |
| e | -17 | -17 <= x < -16 | [-17,-16) |

Endpoint proof for every row: at the left endpoint x=n the floor is n;
at the right endpoint x=n+1 the floor is n+1 and does not satisfy the
condition. Every real point between them has floor n. Integers alone would
omit the noninteger solutions; both closed endpoints would add an invalid one.

## 661: both solution sets and their number-line construction

Task a: [x]=3 iff 3<=x<4, so the solution set is [3,4).
Task b: [x]=-3 iff -3<=x<-2, so the solution set is [-3,-2).

Full construction instructions for the written task:
- Draw an oriented real number line with coordinates increasing to the right.
- For a, label 3 and 4; put a filled point at 3 and an open point at 4;
  mark the entire segment between them, with no extension beyond either end.
- For b, label -3 and -2; put a filled point at -3 and an open point at -2;
  mark the entire segment between them, with no extension beyond either end.
  The negative coordinate -3 is to the left of -2.
- If both sets are shown on one line, keep their conditions/labels separate.
  They are not one connected segment, and the question does not request their
  union as a replacement for the two answers.

These filled/open endpoints follow directly from the same interval equivalence.
In particular, -2 fails [x]=-3 even though it is negative.
This records the full construction mathematics without claiming an image was
drawn, supplied by the source, inspected or reviewed.

## 662: both complete numerical triples and the universal comparison

Task a: x=3.7, hence x+4=7.7 and x-12=-8.3.

| Expression argument | Bound for floor | Floor | Fractional part |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| x=3.7 | 3 <= 3.7 < 4 | 3 | 3.7-3 = 0.7 |
| x+4=7.7 | 7 <= 7.7 < 8 | 7 | 7.7-7 = 0.7 |
| x-12=-8.3 | -9 <= -8.3 < -8 | -9 | -8.3-(-9) = 0.7 |

Complete result in requested order: ({x},{x+4},{x-12})=(0.7,0.7,0.7).

Task b: x=-7.4, hence x-50=-57.4 and x+13=5.6.

| Expression argument | Bound for floor | Floor | Fractional part |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| x=-7.4 | -8 <= -7.4 < -7 | -8 | -7.4-(-8) = 0.6 |
| x-50=-57.4 | -58 <= -57.4 < -57 | -58 | -57.4-(-58) = 0.6 |
| x+13=5.6 | 5 <= 5.6 < 6 | 5 | 5.6-5 = 0.6 |

Complete result in requested order: ({x},{x-50},{x+13})=(0.6,0.6,0.6).

Task c: for every real x and every integer n, {x+n}={x}.
Independent proof, not inference from the two sample triples:
Let m=[x]. Then m<=x<m+1. Adding n gives m+n<=x+n<m+n+1.
Since m+n is an integer, [x+n]=m+n=[x]+n. Therefore
{x+n}=(x+n)-[x+n]=(x+n)-([x]+n)=x-[x]={x}.

The proof includes x an integer, x negative, n=0 and positive/negative n;
there is no sign or nonintegrality restriction. The upper endpoint stays strict
when adding n. This is invariance of fractional part under an integer shift,
not a claim that the floor is unchanged. A noninteger shift is not covered by
the quantifier: for instance {0+1/2}=1/2 differs from {0}=0.
The counterexample clarifies the domain of the proven assertion; it is not
a choice alternative or a newly substituted source task.

## 663: several examples for all three fractional-part targets

Task: produce several numbers with each required fractional part, including
positive, negative and zero/integer behavior where applicable.

For any r in [0,1), {x}=r iff x=k+r for some k in Z:
Necessity follows from x=[x]+{x}. For sufficiency,
k<=k+r<k+1, hence [k+r]=k and {k+r}=r.
Thus the full families here are discrete integer translates, not intervals.

| Part | Required r | Complete admissible family | Several valid examples |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | 0.9 | {k+0.9 : k in Z} | 0.9, 1.9, -0.1, -1.1 |
| b | 0.02 | {k+0.02 : k in Z} | 0.02, 1.02, -0.98, -1.98 |
| c | 0 | Z | -2, -1, 0, 1, 2 |

Verification of a: the floors of the four examples are 0,1,-1,-2;
subtracting yields 0.9 in every case. In particular -0.1-(-1)=0.9.
Verification of b: the floors are 0,1,-1,-2; subtracting yields 0.02
in every case. In particular -1.98-(-2)=0.02.
Verification of c: each example is an integer, so subtracting itself gives 0.
Conversely, {x}=0 implies x=[x] is an integer, so there are no noninteger
solutions for c. There is no restriction to positive integers or to x in [0,1).
The finite lists are witnesses, not unique answers or exhaustive sets.
These are independently generated source solutions, not choice alternatives.

## 664: full graph-construction mathematics and all three comparisons

Original task has four substantive components:
1. Sketch BOTH y=0.7^x and y=0.4^x on comparable axes.
2. Use their intersection to solve 0.7^x=0.4^x.
3. Use their vertical order to solve 0.7^x>0.4^x.
4. Use the opposite vertical order to solve 0.7^x<0.4^x.

This task is exponential-graph comparison, not a floor or fractional-part
exercise merely because it appears in N33. Both bases and all comparisons are
retained exactly. No supplied source figure exists; no final modern figure is
assumed and no source image has been invented.

### Domain, shape, exact anchors and construction

Define f(x)=(7/10)^x and g(x)=(2/5)^x. Each is defined for every x in R,
strictly positive, continuous and strictly decreasing because its base is
strictly between 0 and 1. Each has range (0,+infinity).
At x=0 both equal 1. As x tends to +infinity, each tends to 0 from above;
the horizontal asymptote y=0 is never reached at any finite x.
As x tends to -infinity, each tends to +infinity.
Their graphs have no x-axis intercept, and their y-axis intercept is (0,1).
They are smooth exponential curves, not straight segments or step graphs.
Their upward-convex shape is consistent with
a^((u+v)/2)=sqrt(a^u*a^v)<(a^u+a^v)/2 for u!=v and 0<a<1.

Exact independently computed anchors for BOTH curves:

| x | f(x)=0.7^x | g(x)=0.4^x |
| --- | --- | --- |
| -2 | 100/49 | 25/4 |
| -1 | 10/7 | 5/2 |
| 0 | 1 | 1 |
| 1 | 7/10 | 2/5 |
| 2 | 49/100 | 4/25 |

Negative exponents are reciprocals: (7/10)^(-1)=10/7 and
(2/5)^(-2)=25/4. Positive squares are 49/100 and 4/25.
These anchors are exact reference coordinates for constructing a sketch,
not constraints on an unchosen final viewport, scale, candidate or asset.

Full written construction:
- Use a shared Cartesian coordinate system with x horizontal and y vertical,
  both coordinates increasing in their positive directions.
- Mark the common point (0,1). Place each curve's positive and negative
  reference points using the same coordinate scales; distinguish the two
  curves by their actual function labels.
- Connect each curve's reference points by its smooth decreasing,
  upward-convex exponential shape, continuing upward to the left and toward
  y=0 from above to the right.
- On the positive-x side, keep f strictly above g; on the negative-x side,
  keep f strictly below g. The only shared point is (0,1).
- Do not terminate the mathematical domain at the edge of a finite sketch,
  put an artificial x-axis intercept on either curve, omit the negative-x
  side, or infer an extra intersection from sparse anchors.

### Global comparison and exact solution sets

For every real x, g(x)>0, so division by g(x) preserves equality and the
direction of either inequality. The exact ratio is
f(x)/g(x)=((7/10)/(2/5))^x=(7/4)^x.
Because 7/4>1, its exponential is strictly increasing and equals 1 exactly
at x=0. Therefore:

| x condition | Ratio (7/4)^x | Relative graph heights |
| --- | --- | --- |
| x<0 | <1 | f(x)<g(x) |
| x=0 | =1 | f(x)=g(x)=1 |
| x>0 | >1 | f(x)>g(x) |

This proves the global order and uniqueness of the intersection, rather than
assuming five plotted points prove there are no further crossings.

Equation 0.7^x=0.4^x: x=0; complete solution set {0}.
Strict inequality 0.7^x>0.4^x: x>0; complete solution set (0,+infinity).
Strict inequality 0.7^x<0.4^x: x<0; complete solution set (-infinity,0).
The two strict solution sets exclude zero; at zero there is equality.
They contain all real positive/negative values, not just integers.
The common intersection (0,1) yields the equation's x-coordinate 0, not y=1
as an answer for x. Positive and negative exponents reverse the comparison
between the two bases even though both functions themselves decrease.

The algebraic ratio is an independent mathematical check of the graph-based
solutions. It does not replace the source's sketch-and-read task in coverage.
No rendered drawing or recognition item has been created or reviewed here.

